rsh-nod/lark/zipball/native-softmax-evidence-20261111","body":"Independent full three-pass llm.c softmax study for AMD gfx950.\n\nSource checked by CI: 8ad3fdadb0605ddfbf85e9eb57ce674b3f3024f3. Evidence/metadata commit: 96b75ee3e06dfd3b298af404767762e377a358e4. All four CI jobs passed: https://github.com/harsh-nod/lark/actions/runs/42998373043\n\nBoth the original clamped-read source and guarded repair retain all four barriers and both private eight-element arrays. The corrected abstract infinity seed is executable by the unchanged machine. Fresh Lean builds audit 52 declarations and reject ten false claims. Complete finite address traces expose the original conflict. Whole-launch reachability/reference proofs remain open; this development profile issues zero source certificates.\n\nEach AMD host ran 25 paired trials /50 launches, including the full pinned upstream-default 8192-row,50257-column benchmark. Each host passed1,018,206,820 numerical comparisons across the two variants with zero tolerance failures or nonfinite outputs. Direct comparisons with the declared Torch FP32 reference also pass the recorded tolerance. The four host/method output streams are byte-identical within each dataset.\n\nThe four assets losslessly reconstruct the sampled and upstream-default input/reference data and every original GPU output stream. Per-host original manifests, compiler inputs, numerical audits and successful independent restoration receipts are in reproduction/production_softmax/results/{sampled,upstream}-gpu-v1 and neighboring packets. File sizes and SHA256 digests are pinned in the packets.\n\nDownload the assets into a directory, then run reproduction/production_softmax/restore_gpu.py with --packet reproduction/production_softmax/results/sampled-gpu-v1 or upstream-gpu-v1, --assets that-directory and a fresh --output. audit_gpu.py and audit_torch_gpu.py independently recheck the restored data on CPU.\n\nThese are independent FP16 AMD adaptations. The authors' unreleased source, historical model runs/token costs, secondary32-thread launch and NVIDIA measurements remain unreproduced. No paid model calls were used.\n"}